  ("Glossary. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 355" 0.1429 0.8571 0.4962 0.5179 0.5179 0.0217 0.0038 0.2800 0.0707)
  ("Bibliography. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 359" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5343 0.5560 0.5560 0.0217 0.0038 0.3236 0.1056)
  ("Index. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 367" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5724 0.5941 0.5941 0.0217 0.0038 0.2953 0.0490)
  ("Table of Contents" 0.7052 0.8074 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 0.0028 0.2786 0.0322)
  ("|" 0.8253 0.8290 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil nil 0.0038)
  ("ix" 0.8469 0.8571 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil nil 0.0103))
  (16
  ("" nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil))
  (17
  ("Preface" 0.7348 0.8571 0.1075 0.1532 0.1532 0.0457 nil 0.1954 0.1223)
  ("Data Science for Business is intended for several sorts of readers:" 0.1429 0.6748 0.3238 0.3453 0.3453 0.0214 0.0047 0.2863 0.0416)
  ("• Business people who will be working with data scientists, managing data science–" 0.1600 0.8571 0.3580 0.3794 0.3794 0.0214 0.0049 0.3302 0.0081)
  ("oriented projects, or investing in data science ventures," 0.1786 0.6349 0.3771 0.3985 0.3985 0.0214 0.0047 0.2732 0.0700)
  ("• Developers who will be implementing data science solutions, and" 0.1600 0.7210 0.4022 0.4236 0.4236 0.0214 0.0047 0.3547 0.0081)
  ("• Aspiring data scientists." 0.1600 0.3765 0.4272 0.4487 0.4487 0.0214 0.0047 0.2890 0.0081)
  ("This is not a book about algorithms, nor is it a replacement for a book about algorithms." 0.1429 0.8571 0.4614 0.4828 0.4828 0.0214 0.0035 0.3984 0.0372)
  ("We deliberately avoided an algorithm-centered approach. We believe there is a relatively" 0.1429 0.8571 0.4805 0.5019 0.5019 0.0214 0.0031 0.3806 0.0271)
  ("small set of fundamental concepts or principles that underlie techniques for extracting" 0.1429 0.8572 0.4995 0.5209 0.5209 0.0214 0.0044 0.3295 0.0444)
  ("useful knowledge from data. These concepts serve as the foundation for many well-" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5186 0.5400 0.5400 0.0214 0.0066 0.2840 0.0501)
  ("known algorithms of data mining. Moreover, these concepts underlie the analysis of" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5376 0.5590 0.5590 0.0214 0.0062 0.3142 0.0580)
  ("data-centered business problems, the creation and evaluation of data science solutions," 0.1428 0.8571 0.5566 0.5781 0.5781 0.0214 0.0041 0.3073 0.1141)
  ("and the evaluation of general data science strategies and proposals. Accordingly, we" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5757 0.5971 0.5971 0.0214 0.0067 0.2483 0.0315)
  ("organized the exposition around these general principles rather than around specific" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5947 0.6162 0.6162 0.0214 0.0058 0.2450 0.0828)
  ("algorithms. Where necessary to describe procedural details, we use a combination of" 0.1429 0.8571 0.6138 0.6352 0.6352 0.0214 0.0057 0.3253 0.0950)
  ("text and diagrams, which we think are more accessible than a listing of detailed algo‐" 0.1429 0.8572 0.6328 0.6543 0.6543 0.0214 0.0054 0.3238 0.0314)
  ("rithmic steps." 0.1429 0.2560 0.6519 0.6733 0.6733 0.0214 0.0047 0.3769 0.0622)
  ("The book does not presume a sophisticated mathematical background. However, by its" 0.1429 0.8571 0.6800 0.7014 0.7014 0.0214 0.0041 0.3545 0.0328)
  ("very nature the material is somewhat technical—the goal is to impart a significant un‐" 0.1429 0.8571 0.6991 0.7205 0.7205 0.0214 0.0048 0.3681 0.0364)
  ("derstanding of data science, not just to give a high-level overview. In general, we have" 0.1428 0.8571 0.7181 0.7395 0.7395 0.0214 0.0051 0.2934 0.0999)
  ("tried to minimize the mathematics and make the exposition as “conceptual” as possible." 0.1429 0.8571 0.7371 0.7586 0.7586 0.0214 0.0036 0.3931 0.0395)
  ("Colleagues in industry comment that the book is invaluable for helping to align the" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7653 0.7867 0.7867 0.0214 0.0065 0.2966 0.0903)
  ("understanding of the business, technical/development, and data science teams. That" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7843 0.8057 0.8057 0.0214 0.0065 0.2815 0.1223)
  ("observation is based on a small sample, so we are curious to see how general it truly is" 0.1429 0.8572 0.8034 0.8248 0.8248 0.0214 0.0049 0.3411 0.0984)
  ("(see Chapter 5!). Ideally, we envision a book that any data scientist would give to his" 0.1429 0.8571 0.8224 0.8438 0.8438 0.0214 0.0060 0.2819 0.0326)
  ("collaborators from the development or business teams, effectively saying: if you really" 0.1429 0.8571 0.8415 0.8629 0.8629 0.0214 0.0051 0.3287 0.1092)
  ("xi" 0.8469 0.8571 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil nil 0.0103))
  (18
  ("want to design/implement top-notch data science solutions to business problems, we" 0.1429 0.8571 0.0791 0.1005 0.1005 0.0214 0.0056 0.3239 0.0408)
  ("all need to have a common understanding of this material." 0.1429 0.6286 0.0981 0.1195 0.1195 0.0214 0.0047 0.3599 0.0197)
  ("Colleagues also tell us that the book has been quite useful in an unforeseen way: for" 0.1429 0.8571 0.1262 0.1476 0.1476 0.0214 0.0062 0.2697 0.0903)
  ("preparing to interview data science job candidates. The demand from business for hiring" 0.1429 0.8572 0.1453 0.1667 0.1667 0.0214 0.0027 0.3884 0.0820)
  ("data scientists is strong and increasing. In response, more and more job seekers are" 0.1429 0.8571 0.1643 0.1857 0.1857 0.0214 0.0066 0.2690 0.0353)
  ("presenting themselves as data scientists. Every data science job candidate should un‐" 0.1429 0.8571 0.1834 0.2048 0.2048 0.0214 0.0060 0.2630 0.0881)
  ("derstand the fundamentals presented in this book. (Our industry colleagues tell us that" 0.1428 0.8571 0.2024 0.2238 0.2238 0.0214 0.0042 0.3334 0.0731)
  ("they are surprised how many do not. We have half-seriously discussed a follow-up" 0.1429 0.8571 0.2215 0.2429 0.2429 0.0214 0.0074 0.2772 0.0359)
  ("pamphlet “Cliff ’s Notes to Interviewing for Data Science Jobs.”)" 0.1429 0.6685 0.2405 0.2619 0.2619 0.0214 0.0047 0.3808 0.0792)
  ("Our Conceptual Approach to Data Science" 0.1429 0.6468 0.2768 0.3110 0.3110 0.0343 0.0059 0.2884 0.0442)
  ("In this book we introduce a collection of the most important fundamental concepts of" 0.1428 0.8565 0.3175 0.3389 0.3389 0.0214 0.0047 0.3440 0.0185)
  ("data science. Some of these concepts are “headliners” for chapters, and others are in‐" 0.1428 0.8571 0.3366 0.3580 0.3580 0.0214 0.0056 0.2653 0.0353)
  ("troduced more naturally through the discussions (and thus they are not necessarily" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3556 0.3770 0.3770 0.0214 0.0069 0.2417 0.0754)
  ("labeled as fundamental concepts). The concepts span the process from envisioning the" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3747 0.3961 0.3961 0.0214 0.0044 0.3233 0.0590)
  ("problem, to applying data science techniques, to deploying the results to improve" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3937 0.4151 0.4151 0.0214 0.0083 0.2624 0.0758)
  ("decision-making. The concepts also undergird a large array of business analytics meth‐" 0.1429 0.8572 0.4128 0.4342 0.4342 0.0214 0.0039 0.3357 0.1449)
  ("ods and techniques." 0.1429 0.3082 0.4318 0.4532 0.4532 0.0214 0.0047 0.2539 0.0293)
  ("The concepts fit into three general types:" 0.1429 0.4812 0.4599 0.4813 0.4813 0.0214 0.0047 0.2841 0.0328)
  ("1. Concepts about how data science fits in the organization and the competitive land‐" 0.1534 0.8571 0.4941 0.5155 0.5155 0.0214 0.0038 0.3432 0.0148)
  ("scape, including ways to attract, structure, and nurture data science teams; ways for" 0.1786 0.8571 0.5131 0.5346 0.5346 0.0214 0.0037 0.3504 0.0497)
  ("thinking about how data science leads to competitive advantage; and tactical con‐" 0.1786 0.8571 0.5322 0.5536 0.5536 0.0214 0.0048 0.3035 0.0715)
  ("cepts for doing well with data science projects." 0.1786 0.5644 0.5512 0.5727 0.5727 0.0214 0.0047 0.3152 0.0426)
  ("2. General ways of thinking data-analytically. These help in identifying appropriate" 0.1534 0.8571 0.5763 0.5977 0.5977 0.0214 0.0059 0.2876 0.0148)
  ("data and consider appropriate methods. The concepts include the data mining pro‐" 0.1786 0.8571 0.5954 0.6168 0.6168 0.0214 0.0037 0.3431 0.0352)
  ("cess as well as the collection of different high-level data mining tasks." 0.1786 0.7450 0.6144 0.6358 0.6358 0.0214 0.0047 0.3219 0.0303)
  ("3. General concepts for actually extracting knowledge from data, which undergird the" 0.1534 0.8571 0.6395 0.6609 0.6609 0.0214 0.0033 0.3556 0.0147)
  ("vast array of data science tasks and their algorithms." 0.1786 0.6115 0.6586 0.6800 0.6800 0.0214 0.0047 0.2997 0.0328)
  ("For example, one fundamental concept is that of determining the similarity of two" 0.1429 0.8572 0.6927 0.7141 0.7141 0.0214 0.0073 0.3138 0.0290)
  ("entities described by data. This ability forms the basis for various specific tasks. It may" 0.1429 0.8572 0.7118 0.7332 0.7332 0.0214 0.0047 0.3306 0.0602)
  ("be used directly to find customers similar to a given customer. It forms the core of several" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7308 0.7522 0.7522 0.0214 0.0030 0.4207 0.0194)
  ("prediction algorithms that estimate a target value such as the expected resouce usage of" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7499 0.7713 0.7713 0.0214 0.0043 0.3182 0.0833)
  ("a client or the probability of a customer to respond to an offer. It is also the basis for" 0.1429 0.8572 0.7689 0.7903 0.7903 0.0214 0.0057 0.2899 0.0091)
  ("clustering techniques, which group entities by their shared features without a focused" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7880 0.8094 0.8094 0.0214 0.0054 0.2783 0.0791)
  ("objective. Similarity forms the basis of information retrieval, in which documents or" 0.1428 0.8571 0.8070 0.8284 0.8284 0.0214 0.0062 0.3371 0.0794)
  ("webpages relevant to a search query are retrieved. Finally, it underlies several common" 0.1429 0.8571 0.8261 0.8475 0.8475 0.0214 0.0043 0.3290 0.0800)
  ("algorithms for recommendation. A traditional algorithm-oriented book might present" 0.1429 0.8571 0.8451 0.8665 0.8665 0.0214 0.0049 0.3491 0.0903)
  ("each of these tasks in a different chapter, under different names, with common aspects" 0.1429 0.8571 0.8642 0.8856 0.8856 0.0214 0.0045 0.3496 0.0379)
  ("xii" 0.1429 0.1568 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil 0.2869 0.0139)
  ("| Preface" 0.1746 0.2402 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 0.0179 0.5723 0.0038))
  (19
  ("buried in algorithm details or mathematical propositions. In this book we instead focus" 0.1429 0.8571 0.0791 0.1005 0.1005 0.0214 0.0038 0.3785 0.0548)
  ("on the unifying concepts, presenting specific tasks and algorithms as natural manifes‐" 0.1428 0.8571 0.0981 0.1195 0.1195 0.0214 0.0049 0.3148 0.0220)
  ("tations of them." 0.1429 0.2740 0.1172 0.1386 0.1386 0.0214 0.0047 0.3812 0.0567)
  ("As another example, in evaluating the utility of a pattern, we see a notion of lift— how" 0.1429 0.8572 0.1453 0.1667 0.1667 0.0214 0.0046 0.3733 0.0220)
  ("much more prevalent a pattern is than would be expected by chance—recurring broadly" 0.1429 0.8571 0.1643 0.1857 0.1857 0.0214 0.0032 0.3748 0.0477)
  ("across data science. It is used to evaluate very different sorts of patterns in different" 0.1429 0.8571 0.1834 0.2048 0.2048 0.0214 0.0066 0.2241 0.0516)
  ("contexts. Algorithms for targeting advertisements are evaluated by computing the lift" 0.1429 0.8571 0.2024 0.2238 0.2238 0.0214 0.0054 0.3193 0.0742)
  ("one gets for the targeted population. Lift is used to judge the weight of evidence for or" 0.1429 0.8572 0.2215 0.2429 0.2429 0.0214 0.0048 0.3053 0.0309)
  ("against a conclusion. Lift helps determine whether a co-occurrence (an association) in" 0.1429 0.8571 0.2405 0.2619 0.2619 0.0214 0.0046 0.3078 0.0590)
  ("data is interesting, as opposed to simply being a natural consequence of popularity." 0.1429 0.8311 0.2596 0.2810 0.2810 0.0214 0.0047 0.3041 0.0353)
  ("We believe that explaining data science around such fundamental concepts not only" 0.1429 0.8571 0.2877 0.3091 0.3091 0.0214 0.0063 0.2817 0.0271)
  ("aids the reader, it also facilitates communication between business stakeholders and" 0.1428 0.8571 0.3067 0.3281 0.3281 0.0214 0.0066 0.2851 0.0334)
  ("data scientists. It provides a shared vocabulary and enables both parties to understand" 0.1429 0.8573 0.3258 0.3472 0.3472 0.0214 0.0047 0.2734 0.0353)
  ("each other better. The shared concepts lead to deeper discussions that may uncover" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3448 0.3662 0.3662 0.0214 0.0066 0.2406 0.0379)
  ("critical issues otherwise missed." 0.1429 0.4070 0.3639 0.3853 0.3853 0.0214 0.0047 0.3356 0.0573)
  ("To the Instructor" 0.1429 0.3460 0.4001 0.4344 0.4344 0.0343 0.0059 0.2776 0.0275)
  ("This book has been used successfully as a textbook for a very wide variety of data science" 0.1429 0.8571 0.4409 0.4623 0.4623 0.0214 0.0033 0.3488 0.0372)
  ("courses. Historically, the book arose from the development of Foster’s multidisciplinary" 0.1428 0.8571 0.4599 0.4813 0.4813 0.0214 0.0035 0.3839 0.0671)
  ("Data Science classes at the Stern School at NYU, starting in the fall of 2005.1 The original" 0.1429 0.8571 0.4790 0.5004 0.5004 0.0214 0.0034 0.3834 0.0396)
  ("class was nominally for MBA students and MSIS students, but drew students from" 0.1429 0.8572 0.4980 0.5194 0.5194 0.0214 0.0073 0.3123 0.0385)
  ("schools across the university. The most interesting aspect of the class was not that it" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5171 0.5385 0.5385 0.0214 0.0062 0.2756 0.0617)
  ("appealed to MBA and MSIS students, for whom it was designed. More interesting, it" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5361 0.5575 0.5575 0.0214 0.0059 0.3602 0.0737)
  ("also was found to be very valuable by students with strong backgrounds in machine" 0.1428 0.8571 0.5552 0.5766 0.5766 0.0214 0.0062 0.2699 0.0327)
  ("learning and other technical disciplines. Part of the reason seemed to be that the focus" 0.1429 0.8578 0.5742 0.5956 0.5956 0.0214 0.0047 0.3113 0.0691)
  ("on fundamental principles and other issues besides algorithms was missing from their" 0.1429 0.8571 0.5933 0.6147 0.6147 0.0214 0.0046 0.3578 0.0220)
  ("curricula." 0.1429 0.2228 0.6123 0.6337 0.6337 0.0214 nil 0.2315 0.0800)
  ("At NYU we now use the book in support of a variety of data science–related programs:" 0.1429 0.8571 0.6404 0.6618 0.6618 0.0214 0.0043 0.3520 0.0199)
  ("the original MBA and MSIS programs, undergraduate business analytics, NYU/Stern’s" 0.1429 0.8571 0.6595 0.6809 0.6809 0.0214 0.0046 0.3662 0.0263)
  ("new MS in Business Analytics program, and as the Introduction to Data Science for" 0.1429 0.8572 0.6785 0.6999 0.6999 0.0214 0.0062 0.3114 0.0345)
  ("NYU’s new MS in Data Science. In addition, (prior to publication) the book has been" 0.1429 0.8571 0.6976 0.7190 0.7190 0.0214 0.0053 0.3521 0.0543)
  ("adopted by more than a dozen other universities for programs in seven countries (and" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7166 0.7380 0.7380 0.0214 0.0045 0.3153 0.0679)
  ("counting), in business schools, in computer science programs, and for more general" 0.1428 0.8571 0.7357 0.7571 0.7571 0.0214 0.0063 0.2935 0.0865)
  ("introductions to data science." 0.1429 0.3878 0.7547 0.7761 0.7761 0.0214 0.0047 0.2690 0.1138)
  ("Stay tuned to the books’ websites (see below) for information on how to obtain helpful" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7828 0.8042 0.8042 0.0214 0.0044 0.3441 0.0346)
  ("instructional material, including lecture slides, sample homework questions and prob‐" 0.1429 0.8571 0.8019 0.8233 0.8233 0.0214 0.0043 0.3527 0.1070)
  ("1. Of course, each author has the distinct impression that he did the majority of the work on the book." 0.1594 0.8097 0.8707 0.8870 0.8870 0.0163 0.0036 0.3396 0.0112)
  ("Preface" 0.7561 0.8001 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil 0.1904 0.0439)
  ("|" 0.8179 0.8217 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil nil 0.0038)
  ("xiii" 0.8396 0.8571 0.9225 0.9388 0.9388 0.0163 nil 0.2991 0.0176))
  (20
  ("lems, example project instructions based on the frameworks from the book, exam ques‐" 0.1429 0.8571 0.0791 0.1005 0.1005 0.0214 0.0035 0.3952 0.0436)
  ("tions, and more to come." 0.1429 0.3510 0.0981 0.1195 0.1195 0.0214 0.0047 0.3915 0.0464)
  ("We keep an up-to-date list of known adoptees on the book’s website." 0.2539 0.7857 0.1521 0.1717 0.1717 0.0196 0.0055 0.2988 0.0247)
  ("Click Who’s Using It at the top." 0.2539 0.4934 0.1696 0.1891 0.1891 0.0196 0.0049 0.4053 0.0401)
  ("Other Skills and Concepts" 0.1428 0.4519 0.2254 0.2596 0.2596 0.0343 0.0059 0.3029 0.0700)
  ("There are many other concepts and skills that a practical data scientist needs to know" 0.1429 0.8571 0.2661 0.2875 0.2875 0.0214 0.0052 0.2878 0.0494)
  ("besides the fundamental principles of data science. These skills and concepts will be" 0.1429 0.8572 0.2852 0.3066 0.3066 0.0214 0.0063 0.2847 0.0602)
  ("discussed in Chapter 1 and Chapter 2. The interested reader is encouraged to visit the" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3042 0.3256 0.3256 0.0214 0.0050 0.2909 0.0793)
  ("book’s website for pointers to material for learning these additional skills and concepts" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3233 0.3447 0.3447 0.0214 0.0044 0.3308 0.0516)
  ("(for example, scripting in Python, Unix command-line processing, datafiles, common" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3423 0.3637 0.3637 0.0214 0.0049 0.3690 0.0317)
  ("data formats, databases and querying, big data architectures and systems like MapRe‐" 0.1429 0.8571 0.3613 0.3828 0.3828 0.0214 0.0052 0.3194 0.0353)
  ("duce and Hadoop, data visualization, and other related topics)." 0.1429 0.6642 0.3804 0.4018 0.4018 0.0214 0.0047 0.3008 0.0397)
  ("Sections and Notation" 0.1429 0.4147 0.4167 0.4509 0.4509 0.0343 0.0059 0.2832 0.1034)
  ("In addition to occasional footnotes, the book contains boxed “sidebars.” These are es‐" 0.1429 0.8571 0.4574 0.4788 0.4788 0.0214 0.0053 0.2780 0.0185)
  ("sentially extended footnotes. We reserve these for material that we consider interesting" 0.1429 0.8571 0.4765 0.4979 0.4979 0.0214 0.0042 0.3370 0.0687)
  ("and worthwhile, but too long for a footnote and too much of a digression for the main" 0.1428 0.8571 0.4955 0.5169 0.5169 0.0214 0.0046 0.3560 0.0315)
  ("text." 0.1429 0.1790 0.5146 0.5360 0.5360 0.0214 nil 0.1955 0.0361)
  ("A note on the starred, “curvy road” sections" 0.2371 0.5501 0.5572 0.5771 0.5771 0.0199 0.0040 0.2728 0.0098)
  ("The occasional mathematical details are relegated to optional “starred”" 0.2371 0.7857 0.5765 0.5960 0.5960 0.0196 0.0059 0.2861 0.0300)
  ("sections. These section titles will have asterisk prefixes, and they will" 0.2371 0.7857 0.5939 0.6134 0.6134 0.0196 0.0073 0.2595 0.0655)
  ("include the “curvy road” graphic you see to the left to indicate that the" 0.2371 0.7857 0.6113 0.6309 0.6309 0.0196 0.0056 0.2470 0.0567)
  ("section contains more detailed mathematics or technical details than" 0.2371 0.7857 0.6287 0.6483 0.6483 0.0196 0.0078 0.2884 0.0542)
  ("elsewhere. The book is written so that these sections may be skipped" 0.2371 0.7857 0.6461 0.6657 0.6657 0.0196 0.0070 0.2583 0.0788)
  ("without loss of continuity, although in a few places we remind readers" 0.2371 0.7857 0.6635 0.6831 0.6831 0.0196 0.0059 0.3002 0.0598)
  ("that details appear there." 0.2371 0.4259 0.6809 0.7005 0.7005 0.0196 0.0049 0.2442 0.0298)
  ("Constructions in the text like (Smith and Jones, 2003) indicate a reference to an entry" 0.1429 0.8572 0.7269 0.7483 0.7483 0.0214 0.0050 0.3057 0.1191)
  ("in the bibliography (in this case, the 2003 article or book by Smith and Jones); “Smith" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7459 0.7673 0.7673 0.0214 0.0051 0.3254 0.0170)
  ("and Jones (2003)” is a similar reference. A single bibliography for the entire book appears" 0.1429 0.8571 0.7650 0.7864 0.7864 0.0214 0.0028 0.3797 0.0315)
  ("in the endmatter." 0.1428 0.2854 0.7840 0.8054 0.8054 0.0214 0.0047 0.3655 0.0170)
  ("In this book we try to keep math to a minimum, and what math there is we have sim‐" 0.1428 0.8571 0.8121 0.8335 0.8335 0.0214 0.0050 0.3954 0.0185)
